the pigeonhole principle is a counting  principle that's so obvious that you may  not have noticed that you're using it in  simple form it says that if there are  more pigeons then pigeon holes then you  have to have at least two pigeons in the  same hole okay  we'll get some mileage out of that  shortly but let's remember that this is  actually just an informal way of saying  something that we formerly seen already  one of the mapping rules is that if you  have a total injection from a set a to a  set B that implies that the size of a is  less than or equal to the size of B and  taking the contrapositive of that it  means that if the size of a is greater  than the size of B then no total  injection from A to B as possible no  total injection means that there's no  relation that has an arrow out of  everything in a and at most one arrow in  to be if everything out of a has an  arrow out of it there have to be at  least two arrows two pigeons coming to  the same pigeon hole in B so we know  this rule already and the only thing  that's surprising about it is how you  make use of it we're not gonna make  elaborate uses of it in this little  video you can read in the text about  some amusing applications about proving  that there have to be three people in  the Boston area with more than 10,000  hairs in their heads but the exact same  number or that there have to be two  different subsets of of ninety numbers  of twenty-five digits that have the same  sum but we will take a much more modest  application of the pigeon holing  principle namely  if I have a set of five cards then I  have to have at least two cards with the  same suit why well there are four suits  spades hearts diamonds clubs indicated  here and if you have five cards there's  more pigeons cards than suits holes so  if you're going to assign a pigeon to a  hole again the pigeons are gonna have to  crowd up they're gonna have to be at  least two pigeons in the same  all at least two cards of the same suit  maybe more okay  it's like generalization suppose I have  ten cards how many cards must I have of  the same suit what number of cards at  the same surah my guaranteed to have no  matter what the ten cards are well now  if I have the four slots that I'm trying  to distribute ten cards is it possible  that I had less than three cards in  every hole no because if I have only two  cards in every hole then I have at most  eight elements and I got ten to  distribute in the four slots I have to  bunch them up and pen have at least  three cards of the same suit you can  check that I needn't have any more of  course so the reasoning here is that the  number of cards with the same suit is  gonna be what you get by dividing up the  ten cards that you have by the four  slots and argue that at least one of the  slots has to have an average number of  cards namely ten over four they can't  all be below average and of course since  there are an integer number of cards you  can round up this remember these corner  braces mean round up to the nearest  integer so 10 divided by four rounded up  is three and that's a lower bound on the  number of cards that you have to bunch  up in one slot well generally if I have  n pigeons and I'm gonna be assigning  pigeons to unique holes and if I have H  holes then some hole has to have n  divided by H rounded up again and  divided by H can be understood as the  average number of pigeons per hole and  it's the pigeonhole principle can be  formulated as saying at least one hole  has to have greater than or equal to the  average number and that is the  generalized pigeonhole principle
 

